# Spark Notes — environment
#
# Copy to `.env` in this same directory (the repository root) and edit:
#
#     cp .env.example .env
#
# Every setting here has a working default, so an empty `.env` — or none at
# all — still runs. Uncomment only what you want to change. A variable already
# set in your real shell environment wins over this file, so a one-off
# `PORT=4000 npm run dev` still works.
#
# `.env` is gitignored. Do not commit it.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Where your notes live
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The notes directory. THIS IS THE DATABASE — a plain folder of markdown, with
# no index and nothing else to keep in step. Point it at notes you already have
# and Spark will read them as they are.
# Relative paths resolve against the repository root.
#SPARK_SPACE=./space
#SPARK_SPACE=~/Documents/notes

# Server-side state: the GitHub token and the AI key, both at mode 0600.
# Deliberately never inside the space, so sync can't push your credentials.
#SPARK_STATE=./.spark

# Display name for the space, shown in the interface.
#SPARK_SPACE_NAME=Spark

# Server port. In dev the web client is on :3000 and proxies here.
#PORT=3001

# Interface to bind. The default (every interface) makes the app reachable
# from other machines on the network, which is also what a container needs to
# be reachable at all. Set to 127.0.0.1 to restrict it to the local machine.
#HOST=0.0.0.0


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AI  (optional — everything works without it)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# You do not have to set any of this here. Settings → AI in the app writes the
# same configuration to `.spark/ai.json` at mode 0600, and what you save there
# takes precedence over this file. Use these variables when you would rather
# provision the server than click through the UI.
#
# Every AI call is something you triggered. Nothing watches your keystrokes and
# nothing runs on a timer.

# "openai" for anything speaking the OpenAI-compatible API, "anthropic" for the
# Claude Messages API. Left unset, it is inferred from whichever key you set.
#SPARK_AI_PROVIDER=openai

# The key. Only the one matching your provider is used.
#OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-...
#ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-...

# Model id, exactly as your provider names it.
#SPARK_AI_MODEL=gpt-5
#SPARK_AI_MODEL=claude-opus-5

# Base URL. Leave unset for the provider's own. Set it to reach any
# OpenAI-compatible server — OpenRouter, Groq, Together — or a local runtime,
# in which case nothing leaves your machine and no key is needed.
#SPARK_AI_ENDPOINT=https://openrouter.ai/api/v1
#SPARK_AI_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:11434/v1     # Ollama
#SPARK_AI_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:1234/v1      # LM Studio


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Search by meaning  (optional)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Spark always searches your notes by words — that needs nothing set up. Naming
# an embedding model adds searching by meaning, so "what did I decide about
# pricing" finds the paragraph that never says pricing.
#
# It is a separate model because Anthropic serves no embeddings at all, so
# Claude users have to point this half somewhere else. A local runtime is a
# perfectly good answer, and keeps your notes on your machine.
#
# Vectors are cached in `.spark/embeddings.json`, keyed by the text they
# describe, so editing a page re-embeds only what changed. Delete it any time.
#SPARK_EMBED_MODEL=text-embedding-3-small
#SPARK_EMBED_MODEL=nomic-embed-text              # via Ollama

# Where to send embeddings, if not the same place as the chat model.
#SPARK_EMBED_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:11434/v1


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Spark: you, and how it works
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Starting points only. Anything saved from Settings → Spark lands in
# .spark/spark.json and wins over what is here, the same way the AI key does.

# What Spark should call you.
#SPARK_USER_NAME=Alex

# Standing instructions, injected above the rest of the system prompt and
# declared to outrank it. This is the place for "never use bullet points in a
# journal entry" — a rule you want obeyed rather than weighed.
#SPARK_INSTRUCTIONS=Write in British English. Keep replies to a paragraph.

# Web search, through Exa. Without a key the tool is not offered at all, because
# a model told it can search will promise to and then apologise.
#EXA_API_KEY=


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Code sandbox
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Lets Spark run a short Python or JavaScript program to answer the questions
# about a folder of markdown that are arithmetic rather than reading: totalling
# a column, counting across months, reshaping a CSV. A model that reasons its
# way to a total gets it subtly wrong; one that writes six lines of Python gets
# it right, and you can read the six lines.
#
# The runtime lives here rather than in the app's settings on purpose. Whether
# this machine will execute generated code is a fact about the machine, not a
# preference. What is in Settings is your permission — "Run code" is off until
# you turn it on — so there are two gates and this is only the outer one.
#
#   auto     THE DEFAULT. Docker if the daemon answers when the server starts,
#            otherwise the local interpreter. Resolved once, so what the prompt
#            promises and what actually runs cannot disagree.
#   docker   A container with no network and a memory cap. The only host path
#            inside it is the working directory. USE THIS ONE.
#   node     A plain subprocess. The API key is kept out of its environment and
#   python   it is killed on a timer — and that is all. It can read every file
#            the server can, including your whole space, and it can reach the
#            network. A convenience for a laptop with no Docker, not a boundary.
#   off      The tool does not exist.
#
# Anything else is treated as a command template, with {file} and {dir}
# substituted — which is how you point this at E2B, Firecracker or a runner of
# your own without changing any code.
#SPARK_SANDBOX=auto
#SPARK_SANDBOX=docker
#SPARK_SANDBOX=off
#SPARK_SANDBOX=my-runner --exec {dir}/{file}

# Image for the docker runtime. Unset picks a small official one per language.
#SPARK_SANDBOX_IMAGE=python:3.12-alpine

# Milliseconds before a script is killed.
#SPARK_SANDBOX_TIMEOUT=20000

# The agent's own directory, which survives between runs — so a long job can be
# done in steps rather than in one script. Outside the space, because scratch
# work is not notes: three attempts at a chart do not belong in your page list.
#SPARK_SANDBOX_WORKDIR=.spark/workspace

# Set to false to go back to a fresh temporary directory per run.
#SPARK_SANDBOX_PERSIST=true


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GitHub sync  (optional)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Only needed for "Connect GitHub" in the sync panel. Spark runs in online mode
# without any of this; sync mode adds git on top and stays opt-in.
#
# Create an OAuth app at https://github.com/settings/developers with the
# callback URL set to  <SPARK_ORIGIN>/api/auth/github/callback

#GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=
#GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET=

# The public origin, used to build that callback URL. Change it if you are not
# on localhost.
#SPARK_ORIGIN=http://localhost:3001

# Author recorded on sync commits.
#SPARK_GIT_NAME=Spark Notes
#SPARK_GIT_EMAIL=spark@localhost
#SPARK_GIT_BRANCH=main


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rarely needed
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# In production the server also serves the built client from here.
#SPARK_WEB_DIST=apps/web/dist

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Container images (ghcr.io/<you>/<repo>)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `docker run ghcr.io/<you>/<repo>` — every variable in this file works as a
# `-e` or via `--env-file`. Inside the image the defaults are:
#
#   SPARK_SPACE=/data/space   mount your notes here
#   SPARK_STATE=/data/state   GitHub token and AI keys, kept apart from the
#                             space so sync never pushes credentials
#   PORT=3001                 the port to publish with `-p`
#   HOST=0.0.0.0              binds all interfaces
#
# Images do not bundle the curated typefaces (see `npm run fonts`); build with
# `--build-arg FETCH_FONTS=1` to include them.
